WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
The same local filter visits different image regions.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
Its response changes with each region’s content while the weights remain shared.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.550
Learning those filters builds representations from pixels.

4
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:28.900
Split a matrix product into four output blocks.

5
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:35.750
Each compute unit calculates a block using the operands it needs.

6
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:43.100
The output assembles those blocks; moving operands and results also consumes resources.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.450
Unmasked dense attention considers sixteen pairs for four positions.

8
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:57.300
At eight positions, the grid contains sixty-four pairs.

9
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:04.967
This quadratic relation counts interactions; it does not itself predict kernel runtime.

10
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:12.317
In this normalized example, cost is proportional to parameters times data.

11
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:19.167
Doubling parameters requires fewer data at the same fixed budget.

12
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:27.967
The best allocation needs a validated loss law, not an assumption that more parameters always wins.

13
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:35.317
Pretraining builds shared parameters from broad data.

14
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:42.167
The same base can receive instructions or adaptations for different tasks.

15
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:49.517
Reusing the base does not imply equal quality across all tasks.

16
00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:56.867
Predicting continuations and responding appropriately to instructions are not identical objectives.

17
00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:03.717
Response examples and preference signals can guide later training.

18
00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:11.067
That training changes behavior; it does not guarantee every response is true or safe.

